# <%= project.name %> — designs

The designs of <%= project.name %>: its design system, its components and clickable screens of the app. They open in a browser as they are, with no build step. Flosis keeps this repository; the application is built from it.

## Branches

- `master` — all approved designs: the target look, possibly ahead of production;
- `design/<KEY>` — the last approved design of a Jira task;
- `wip/<KEY>` — work in progress, not approved, do not build from it;
- tags `approved/<KEY>/<n>` mark each approval.

`<KEY>` is the task's Jira key, such as `ABC-123`, and `<n>` counts the task's approvals from 1.

## What a task changed

What approval `<n>` of a task changed:

```sh
git diff approved/<KEY>/<n>^1 approved/<KEY>/<n>
```

The task's latest approval, which gives the latest `<n>`:

```sh
git tag -l 'approved/<KEY>/*' --sort=-v:refname | head -1
```

## Run it locally

In the repository's folder:

```sh
python3 -m http.server 8000
```

(or `npx http-server -p 8000 -c-1`), then open http://localhost:8000. `file://` is not supported: the pages load their parts over HTTP. Nor is `npx serve`: it redirects `page.html?ctx=…` to `page` and drops the contexts.

## Structure

| Path | What it holds |
|---|---|
| `README.md` | this file |
| `index.html` | the entry point: links to the design system, the components and the screens |
| `design-system/` | the brand book, the tokens (`theme.css`), assets and fonts |
| `components/` | one folder per component: its guidelines, a preview and the component itself |
| `designs/` | clickable screens of the app, their fake data and the contexts (`contexts.json`) |
| `runtime/` | what the browser needs: `flosis.js` (components and contexts), `nav.html` (the navigation the repo's own pages include) and `config.json`<% if kit == "tailwind4" %>, plus `kit.js` (the Tailwind CSS browser build) and its MIT licence, `kit-license.txt`<% end %> |

Nothing else belongs in the repository.

## Styling

<% if kit == "tailwind4" -%>
Pages use Tailwind CSS v4 utility classes. `runtime/kit.js`, the Tailwind browser build (version <%= Designs::Template::TAILWIND_VERSION %>, vendored), compiles them in the browser with the tokens in `design-system/theme.css`: `--color-primary` there is `bg-primary` or `text-primary` on a page, and `var(--color-primary)` in CSS.
<% else -%>
Pages use plain CSS. The tokens are custom properties in `design-system/theme.css`, which every page gets: `background: var(--color-primary)`.
<% end -%>

## Components

A screen never copies a component's markup. It includes the component:

```html
<x-include src="components/<name>/<name>.html" title="…">children</x-include>
```

- `src` starts at the repository's root, whichever page the include is on.
- In the component, each `{{name}}` placeholder is replaced with the include's attribute of that name, HTML-escaped (here `{{title}}`), or with nothing when the include has no such attribute.
- The include's children take the place of the component's first `<slot></slot>`.
- Components can include components.
- Paths inside a component are relative to the repo root. That holds for its `src`, `href`, `srcset` and `poster` attributes, including paths passed in through a placeholder. It does not hold for the include's children: they belong to the page, and their paths stay relative to the page. A URL with a scheme (`https:`, `mailto:`, `data:`…), and one starting with `/`, `#` or `?`, is left as it is.
- A component that cannot be loaded shows as a red "Missing component" box. A `<script>` in a component does not run: behaviour belongs to the page.

## Contexts

Screens can differ by context: who is looking, or which part of the product is on. `designs/contexts.json` groups the contexts into dimensions, and one value of each dimension is active at a time:

```json
{
  "dimensions": [
    {
      "id": "role",
      "name": "Role",
      "values": [{ "id": "admin", "name": "Admin" }, { "id": "client", "name": "Client" }],
      "default": "admin"
    }
  ]
}
```

- Dimension and value ids are made of letters, digits, `_` and `-` (`[A-Za-z0-9_-]`), and value ids are unique across dimensions.
- The URL names the active values, `?ctx=<value>,<value>`, such as `designs/index.html?ctx=client`. A dimension that the URL leaves out uses its `default`, else its first value.
- Clicking a link within the repo replaces the link's own `?ctx=` with the active contexts, so the contexts stay as they are from page to page. They are switched in the preview's context picker, or by editing `?ctx=` in the address bar, never by a link.
- `data-ctx-show="admin client"` shows an element only while one of the values listed is active; `data-ctx-hide="client"` hides it while one is.
- `<html>` gets a `data-ctx-<dimension>` attribute per dimension, for CSS: `html[data-ctx-role="client"] .price { … }`.
- In a script, wait for `window.Flosis.ready` (or the `flosis:ready` event on `document`), then read `window.Flosis.ctx`: `{ "role": "client" }`.

## For AI assistants

To build a task's design into the application:

1. Start from the Jira issue: its key is the `<KEY>` of the branches and tags above.
2. Check out `design/<KEY>`, never `wip/<KEY>`.
3. Read the screens the task changed (see "What a task changed"), in a browser and in their HTML.
4. Implement them in the application's own stack, with its own components. The HTML here is a reference, not code to copy, and `runtime/` only shows the designs in a browser.
5. Take colours, fonts, radii and the other tokens from `design-system/theme.css`, never from a screen's markup.
